    "result_text": "The implementation is complete and the full test suite passes (22 tests, 0 failures).\n\n## Summary\n\nI implemented the `namesakeCount` field on owner creation \u2014 the count of existing owners who, before this create, already share the same `firstName` and `lastName` (case-insensitive). Following the existing pattern for the other derived-on-create fields (`customerCode`, `householdId`), the value is computed at create time, persisted, and returned on `GET /api/owners/{id}`.\n\nChanges:\n\n- **`src/main/resources/openapi.yml`** \u2014 added `namesakeCount` (readOnly `integer`/int32, `minimum: 0`) to the `Owner` schema, which generates the `getNamesakeCount`/`setNamesakeCount` accessors on `OwnerDto`.\n- **`Owner.java`** \u2014 added the `namesakeCount` column field (`Integer`, `@Column(name = \"namesake_count\")`) with getter/setter. MapStruct maps it into the DTO automatically.\n- **`OwnerRestControllerV1.addOwner`** \u2014 added `countNamesakes(firstName, lastName)`, which iterates `findAllOwners()` and counts case-insensitive matches on both names *before* the new owner is saved, then sets it on the owner via `owner.setNamesakeCount(...)`.\n- **Schema files** (`hsqldb`, `h2`, `mysql`, `postgres`) \u2014 added the `namesake_count` column to the `owners` table.\n\nThe new `Cp13Tests.coreCountsNamesakes` (which creates two owners sharing a name in different households and asserts `namesakeCount == 1` on the second) passes, and all existing tests remain green.",
